M.TOPICS["mobile-apps"] = [[
# Mobile apps

Design phone Screens 390 wide, each a Frame with `clip = true` and a height that grows with its content (`height = "fit_content"`); fix 844 only for a Screen meant to show exactly one viewport. Build the frame of the app first (status bar and tab bar), check it, then the content.

## Structure of a Screen

A vertical Frame holding, in order:

1. **Status bar**: 62 tall, the time on the left and signal and battery on the right, vertically centred, 24 side padding. Nothing else goes in it or under it.
2. **Content**: one wrapper Frame holding everything else: side padding 16–20 set once on it, `gap` 24–32 between major blocks and 12–16 inside related groups, bottom padding equal to the gap. Space comes from gaps and padding, never from empty spacer Frames.
3. **Tab bar**: the last child of the Screen's column, not `absolute`.

```luau
SetTokens({
  bg = { type = "color", value = "#F6F6F4" },
  surface = { type = "color", value = "#FFFFFF" },
  fg = { type = "color", value = "#111111" },
  muted = { type = "color", value = "#76766F" },
  accent = { type = "color", value = "#16A34A" },
  ["accent-soft"] = { type = "color", value = "#DCFCE7" },
})
G.phone = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Home", x = 0, y = 0, width = 390, height = "fit_content", layout = "vertical", fill = "$bg", clip = true, inProgress = true })
Insert(G.phone, { type = "frame", name = "Status bar", width = "fill_container", height = 62, layout = "horizontal", padding = { 0, 24 },
  justifyContent = "space_between", alignItems = "center", children = {
    { type = "text", name = "Time", content = "9:41", fontSize = 16, fontWeight = 600, fill = "$fg" },
    { type = "frame", name = "Indicators", layout = "horizontal", gap = 6, alignItems = "center", children = {
      { type = "rectangle", name = "Signal", width = 18, height = 10, cornerRadius = 2, fill = "$fg" },
      { type = "rectangle", name = "Battery", width = 26, height = 12, cornerRadius = 3, fill = "$fg" },
    } },
  } })
G.content = Insert(G.phone, { type = "frame", name = "Content", width = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 28, padding = { 8, 20, 28, 20 } })
```

## Tab bar

For three to five destinations, a floating capsule reads as modern and keeps content visible around it:

- Inset about 16 from the sides and 12 from the bottom (a wrapper Frame with that padding).
- About 56 tall, radius half the height, about 6 inside padding, items sharing the width.
- A translucent surface (about 70% opaque) with a soft shadow, and a background blur if content shows through.
- Each item: a 22 icon over a 10 label in sentence case.
- The selected item: a filled icon in the accent on a soft accent pill; the others outlined icons in a muted colour. Icon sets with filled and outline pairs make this easy: Phosphor (`ph:house`, `ph:house-fill`), Material Symbols (`material-symbols:home-outline`, `material-symbols:home`). Lucide has no filled icons.

```luau
local function tab(label, icon, selected)
  return { type = "frame", name = `Tab {label}`, width = "fill_container", height = "fill_container",
    layout = "vertical", gap = 2, justifyContent = "center", alignItems = "center", cornerRadius = 999, fill = if selected then "$accent-soft" else "#00000000",
    children = {
      { type = "icon", name = `{label} icon`, icon = if selected then `{icon}-fill` else icon, width = 22, height = 22, fill = if selected then "$accent" else "$muted" },
      { type = "text", name = `{label} label`, content = label, fontSize = 10, fontWeight = if selected then 600 else 500, fill = if selected then "$accent" else "$muted" },
    } }
end
Insert(G.phone, { type = "frame", name = "Tab bar", width = "fill_container", padding = { 0, 16, 12, 16 }, children = {
  { type = "frame", name = "Capsule", width = "fill_container", height = 56, padding = 6, layout = "horizontal", cornerRadius = 28,
    fill = "#FFFFFFB3", effect = { { type = "shadow", offset = { x = 0, y = 6 }, blur = 20, color = "#0000001F" }, { type = "background_blur", radius = 12 } },
    children = { tab("Home", "ph:house", true), tab("Search", "ph:magnifying-glass", false), tab("Saved", "ph:heart", false), tab("Profile", "ph:user", false) } },
} })
TakeScreenshot(G.phone)
```

## Content

- One main intent per Screen. The title is the same size on every Screen (28–34, bold); main actions sit in the lower half, within thumb reach.
- Touch targets are at least 44 × 44, even when the visible icon is smaller: pad the Frame around it.
- Lists are full-width rows (56–72 tall) with a leading icon or avatar, a title and a muted subtitle, and a trailing value or chevron. Dividers are optional when the spacing groups well.
- Cards span the content width (`fill_container`); horizontal carousels show a peek of the next card.
- Text: body 15–17, never under 12; labels in sentence case.

```luau
local function row(title, detail, amount)
  return { type = "frame", name = title, width = "fill_container", height = 64, layout = "horizontal", gap = 12,
    padding = { 0, 16 }, alignItems = "center", children = {
      { type = "ellipse", name = `{title} avatar`, width = 40, height = 40, fill = "$accent-soft" },
      { type = "frame", name = `{title} text`, width = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 2, children = {
        { type = "text", name = `{title} title`, content = title, fontSize = 16, fontWeight = 500, fill = "$fg" },
        { type = "text", name = `{title} detail`, content = detail, fontSize = 13, fill = "$muted" },
      } },
      { type = "text", name = `{title} amount`, content = amount, fontSize = 16, fontWeight = 600, fill = "$fg" },
    } }
end
Insert(G.content, { type = "text", name = "Title", content = "Groceries", fontSize = 32, fontWeight = 700, letterSpacing = -0.5, fill = "$fg" })
Insert(G.content, { type = "frame", name = "Recent", width = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", cornerRadius = 20, fill = "$surface", padding = { 4, 0 },
  children = { row("Greenmarket", "Today, 9:12", "$42.10"), row("Corner Bakery", "Yesterday", "$8.40"), row("FreshBox", "Mon", "$61.00") } })
local issues = {}
Get(G.phone, function(node, info)
  if #info.problems > 0 then table.insert(issues, `{node.name}: ` .. table.concat(info.problems, "; ")) end
  if node.type == "text" and (node.fontSize or 14) < 12 and string.sub(node.name, -5) ~= "label" then
    table.insert(issues, `{node.name}: under 12 px`)
  end
end)
Print(if #issues > 0 then issues else "clean")
Update(G.phone, { inProgress = false })
TakeScreenshot(G.content)
```

## Several Screens

Place each new Screen with `FindEmptySpace({ width = 390, height = 844, from = previousScreen, padding = 80 })`, in the order a person moves through them. Make the status bar and tab bar Components once the second Screen needs them, and give each Screen its own Instance with the right tab selected.
]]

M.TOPICS["web-apps"] = [[
# Web apps

Product UI for people doing work: dashboards, admin tools, editors, settings. It's functional; it leaves marketing moves (big hero type, gradients, testimonials) to landing pages.

## Before the layout

Infer what the product is, then its structure. Answer these first, in a sentence each:

- What's the one dominant region (the list, the canvas, the document, the inbox)? It gets most of the width.
- What's the primary action on this Screen?
- What density suits the work: compact for data-heavy tools, comfortable for most, airy for occasional flows?
- What's hidden until needed (advanced filters, bulk actions, settings)?

Don't reach for a sidebar, a table or a dashboard of cards by habit. Two zones (navigation and content) before adding a third (a detail panel). Whitespace separates zones better than borders; no scroll areas inside scroll areas.

## Skeletons

- **Sidebar and main**: 1440 wide; sidebar about 240–280 fixed; main `fill_container` with 32 padding and 24 gaps.
- **Top bar**: 56–64 tall, `space_between`, a bottom border, the page title or breadcrumbs on the left and actions on the right.
- **Main and side column**: main `fill_container`, side column about 320–360 fixed.
- **Card grids**: rows of equal `fill_container` cards; a grid is a column of rows, since layouts don't wrap.

```luau
SetTokens({
  bg = { type = "color", value = "#FAFAFA" },
  panel = { type = "color", value = "#FFFFFF" },
  border = { type = "color", value = "#E5E5E5" },
  fg = { type = "color", value = "#0A0A0A" },
  muted = { type = "color", value = "#737373" },
  accent = { type = "color", value = "#4F46E5" },
  selected = { type = "color", value = "#F0F0F0" },
})
G.app = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Dashboard", x = 0, y = 0, width = 1440, height = 900, layout = "horizontal", fill = "$bg", clip = true, inProgress = true })
local function navItem(label, icon, active)
  return { type = "frame", name = label, width = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", gap = 10, padding = { 8, 12 },
    cornerRadius = 6, alignItems = "center", fill = if active then "$selected" else "#00000000", children = {
      { type = "icon", name = `{label} icon`, icon = icon, width = 16, height = 16, fill = if active then "$fg" else "$muted" },
      { type = "text", name = `{label} label`, content = label, fontSize = 14, fontWeight = if active then 600 else 400, fill = if active then "$fg" else "$muted" },
    } }
end
Insert(G.app, { type = "frame", name = "Sidebar", width = 248, height = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 2, padding = { 20, 12 },
  fill = "$panel", stroke = { fill = "$border", thickness = { top = 0, right = 1, bottom = 0, left = 0 } }, children = {
    { type = "frame", name = "Brand", width = "fill_container", padding = { 0, 12, 16, 12 }, children = {
      { type = "text", name = "Wordmark", content = "Ledgerly", fontSize = 16, fontWeight = 700, fill = "$fg" },
    } },
    navItem("Overview", "lucide:layout-dashboard", true), navItem("Customers", "lucide:users", false), navItem("Settings", "lucide:settings", false),
  } })
G.main = Insert(G.app, { type = "frame", name = "Main", width = "fill_container", height = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 24, padding = 32 })
Insert(G.main, { type = "frame", name = "Top bar", width = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", justifyContent = "space_between", alignItems = "center", children = {
  { type = "text", name = "Page title", content = "Overview", fontSize = 24, fontWeight = 600, letterSpacing = -0.3, fill = "$fg" },
  { type = "frame", name = "New invoice", layout = "horizontal", padding = { 8, 14 }, cornerRadius = 6, fill = "$accent", children = {
    { type = "text", name = "New invoice label", content = "New invoice", fontSize = 14, fontWeight = 600, fill = "#FFFFFF" },
  } },
} })
```

## Showing things concretely

- Entities show what identifies and matters about them: a name, a status, the two or three facts people scan for, and the actions they take on it.
- Status reads as a small badge in a semantic colour pair, with a word, not colour alone.
- Labels beat icons where meaning matters; icon-only buttons are for universally known actions (search, close, more).
- Metrics: a muted label, a large value, and the change in a semantic colour.

```luau
local function metric(label, value, change, up)
  return { type = "frame", name = label, width = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 6, padding = 20,
    cornerRadius = 10, fill = "$panel", stroke = { fill = "$border", thickness = 1 }, children = {
      { type = "text", name = `{label} label`, content = label, fontSize = 13, fill = "$muted" },
      { type = "text", name = `{label} value`, content = value, fontSize = 30, fontWeight = 600, letterSpacing = -0.5, fill = "$fg" },
      { type = "text", name = `{label} change`, content = change, fontSize = 13, fontWeight = 500, fill = if up then "#15803D" else "#B91C1C" },
    } }
end
local metrics = Insert(G.main, { type = "frame", name = "Metrics", width = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", gap = 16,
  children = { metric("Revenue", "$48,210", "+12.4% vs last month", true), metric("Outstanding", "$6,930", "+3 invoices overdue", false),
    metric("Paid on time", "92%", "+4 points", true) } })
local bars = { 42, 58, 51, 66, 72, 61, 80, 77, 85, 90, 83, 96 }
local barNodes = {}
for i, v in bars do
  table.insert(barNodes, { type = "rectangle", name = `Bar {i}`, width = "fill_container", height = v * 3, cornerRadius = { 4, 4, 0, 0 },
    fill = if i == #bars then "$accent" else "#C7D2FE" })
end
Insert(G.main, { type = "frame", name = "Revenue chart", width = "fill_container", height = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 16, padding = 24,
  cornerRadius = 10, fill = "$panel", stroke = { fill = "$border", thickness = 1 }, children = {
    { type = "text", name = "Chart title", content = "Monthly revenue", fontSize = 15, fontWeight = 600, fill = "$fg" },
    { type = "frame", name = "Bars", width = "fill_container", height = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", gap = 12, alignItems = "end",
      children = barNodes },
  } })
local issues = {}
Get(G.app, function(node, info)
  if #info.problems > 0 then table.insert(issues, `{node.name}: ` .. table.concat(info.problems, "; ")) end
end)
Print(if #issues > 0 then issues else "clean")
Update(G.app, { inProgress = false })
TakeScreenshot(metrics)
```

## States

Design the states, not only the happy path: loading (skeleton shapes in the layout's places), empty (one sentence on what goes here and the action that fills it), error (what happened and what to do), success, and no permission. Copy the Screen for each state and change only what differs. Destructive actions confirm in a dialog; others give immediate feedback.

## Growth and breakpoints

- Design for more: a list of 3 should also work with 300 (pagination or virtual scrolling shown), a nav of 4 with 12.
- Patterns repeat across Screens: the same top bar, the same table, the same empty state.
- Progressive disclosure: advanced options behind a toggle or a menu.
- On a phone the same app is one column: the sidebar becomes a tab bar or a menu, side panels become sheets, tables become cards. Hierarchy survives every breakpoint.
]]

M.TOPICS["slides"] = [[
# Slides

Each slide is a Screen of 1920 × 1080 with `clip = true`, its content kept at least 100 from every edge (`padding = 100` or more). A deck is a row of slides placed in order with `FindEmptySpace`, each named by its number and point (`03 Why now`). Build a slide per call, or two or three when they share a layout.

## Priorities

Clarity first, then readability from the back of a room, then hierarchy, then simplicity.

- **One message per slide.** The title states the takeaway as a sentence ("Churn halved after onboarding changed"), not a topic ("Churn").
- **Short phrases, not paragraphs.** When it doesn't fit, split the slide or cut words. Never shrink the type to make it fit.
- **Type for projection.** Body 28–32 (never under 24), titles 40 and up, line height 1.1–1.2 for display sizes, two families at most. Scale a brand's type up rather than using its web sizes.
- **Colour.** Two or three colours plus neutrals, the accent for the one thing to notice, strong contrast, and pairs that stay distinct for colour-blind viewers (blue and orange rather than red and green).
- **Text-only slides go bold**: oversized type, asymmetric placement, one accent word.

```luau
SetTokens({
  paper = { type = "color", value = "#F4F1EA" },
  ink = { type = "color", value = "#141414" },
  quiet = { type = "color", value = "#6B6760" },
  accent = { type = "color", value = "#2F5BEA" },
  display = { type = "string", value = "Instrument Serif" },
  text = { type = "string", value = "Geist" },
})
local function slide(name, props)
  local spot = FindEmptySpace({ width = 1920, height = 1080, padding = 120 })
  local node = { type = "frame", name = name, x = spot.x, y = spot.y, width = 1920, height = 1080, fill = "$paper", clip = true, padding = 120 }
  for k, v in props do node[k] = v end
  return Insert(node)
end
G.cover = slide("01 Cover", { layout = "vertical", justifyContent = "end", gap = 32, children = {
  { type = "text", name = "Kicker", content = "Q3 review", fontFamily = "$text", fontSize = 28, fontWeight = 500, fill = "$quiet" },
  { type = "text", name = "Title", content = "We halved churn by fixing the first week", fontFamily = "$display", fontSize = 120, lineHeight = 1.0, letterSpacing = -2, fill = "$ink", width = 1400 },
} })
TakeScreenshot(G.cover)
```

## Layouts

- **Cover**: a title of 48–72 (more for a type-led cover), two lines at most.
- **Section break**: a small muted label over a 48–56 title.
- **Statement**: one sentence of 36–48, at most two lines, lots of space.
- **Quote**: 28–36, at most three lines, attribution at 20–24 below.
- **Concept and visual**: a 50/50 split, title 36–40 and at most four lines of body on one side, the visual on the other, at least 40 apart, vertically centred. Or a full-bleed image under a dark scrim with a 40–56 title.
- **Pillars**: three or four equal columns, top-aligned, each a visual or icon, a 28 label and a 20 description, 30–50 apart.
- **Comparison**: two columns 40–60 apart, or before and after with "before" muted.
- **KPIs**: hero numbers at 120–200 for one, 80–120 for two, 64–80 for three, each with a short label.
- **Process**: three to five steps along one line.
- **2 × 2**: four equal cards, 20–30 apart.
- **List**: a 40 title and three to five items at 28.
- **Closing**: 48–56 and the contact details or the ask.

Helpers like `slide` don't carry from one call to the next (`G` holds only plain values, not functions), so each snippet defines the ones it uses.

```luau
local function slide(name, props)
  local spot = FindEmptySpace({ width = 1920, height = 1080, padding = 120 })
  local node = { type = "frame", name = name, x = spot.x, y = spot.y, width = 1920, height = 1080, fill = "$paper", clip = true, padding = 120 }
  for k, v in props do node[k] = v end
  return Insert(node)
end
local function kpi(value, label)
  return { type = "frame", name = label, width = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 16, children = {
    { type = "text", name = `{label} value`, content = value, fontFamily = "$display", fontSize = 180, lineHeight = 1.0, letterSpacing = -4, fill = "$accent" },
    { type = "text", name = `{label} label`, content = label, fontFamily = "$text", fontSize = 30, lineHeight = 1.3, fill = "$ink", width = "fill_container" },
  } }
end
G.results = slide("02 Results", { layout = "vertical", gap = 96, children = {
  { type = "text", name = "Takeaway", content = "Better onboarding moved both numbers that matter", fontFamily = "$display", fontSize = 64, lineHeight = 1.05, fill = "$ink", width = "fill_container" },
  { type = "frame", name = "KPIs", width = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", gap = 120, children = { kpi("-48%", "churn in the first 30 days"), kpi("2.3×", "teams inviting a colleague in week one") } },
} })
TakeScreenshot(G.results)
```

## Charts on slides

One insight per chart. Make the chart about 60% of the slide's height, highlight the bar or segment that carries the point in the accent and the rest in a quiet tone, and put the insight in a bold one-line title. Bars are Frames in a horizontal layout aligned to the bottom; labels sit in the layout under them.

```luau
local function slide(name, props)
  local spot = FindEmptySpace({ width = 1920, height = 1080, padding = 120 })
  local node = { type = "frame", name = name, x = spot.x, y = spot.y, width = 1920, height = 1080, fill = "$paper", clip = true, padding = 120 }
  for k, v in props do node[k] = v end
  return Insert(node)
end
local months = { { "Apr", 9.1 }, { "May", 8.7 }, { "Jun", 8.9 }, { "Jul", 6.2 }, { "Aug", 5.1 }, { "Sep", 4.7 } }
local columns = {}
for i, m in months do
  local month, value = m[1], m[2]
  local after = i >= 4
  table.insert(columns, { type = "frame", name = month, width = "fill_container", layout = "vertical", gap = 16, alignItems = "center", children = {
    { type = "text", name = `{month} value`, content = `{value}%`, fontFamily = "$text", fontSize = 26, fontWeight = 600, fill = if after then "$accent" else "$quiet" },
    { type = "rectangle", name = `{month} bar`, width = "fill_container", height = value * 56, cornerRadius = { 8, 8, 0, 0 }, fill = if after then "$accent" else "#D9D4C9" },
    { type = "text", name = `{month} label`, content = month, fontFamily = "$text", fontSize = 24, fill = "$quiet" },
  } })
end
G.chart = slide("03 Churn", { layout = "vertical", gap = 64, children = {
  { type = "text", name = "Insight", content = "Churn fell from July, when the new first week shipped", fontFamily = "$display", fontSize = 56, fill = "$ink", width = "fill_container" },
  { type = "frame", name = "Chart", width = "fill_container", height = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", gap = 48, alignItems = "end", children = columns },
} })
local issues = {}
Get(G.chart, function(node, info)
  if #info.problems > 0 then table.insert(issues, `{node.name}: ` .. table.concat(info.problems, "; ")) end
end)
Print(if #issues > 0 then issues else "clean")
TakeScreenshot(G.chart)
```

## Matching the context

Corporate decks are structured and restrained; startup pitches minimal and bold; marketing decks lead with benefits; internal reviews can be denser; keynotes lean on visuals and single words. Ask yourself who's in the room, and match it.
]]

M.TOPICS["tables"] = [[
# Tables

A table is Frames all the way down, so its columns line up by construction:

- **Table**: a vertical Frame, `width = "fill_container"` (or fixed), with a border and a radius from the Tokens and `clip = true`.
- **Row**: a horizontal Frame, `width = "fill_container"`, with a bottom border (a stroke with only `bottom` set).
- **Cell**: a Frame in the row with the column's width and `height = "fill_container"`, its padding, and its alignment.
- **Content**: a text, a badge, an avatar, an icon button, inside the cell. Text never sits directly in a row.

The header row is built the same way, with the same column widths, its text muted or bold and smaller.

## Column widths

Fix every column but one, and let that one fill:

- Name or ID: 200–250.
- Email, URL, description: `fill_container`.
- Status: 100–120.
- Dates: 120–150.
- Numbers: 80–120, right-aligned (`justifyContent = "end"`), in a face with even figures.
- Actions: 64–100, right-aligned.

Four to seven columns. More, and the table needs a different design (a detail panel, or a choice of columns).

## Build it from data

Describe the columns once, then build the header and each row from the same description. Use plausible data: real-looking names, varied lengths, a long one to test truncation, numbers with realistic spread.

```luau
SetTokens({
  panel = { type = "color", value = "#FFFFFF" },
  line = { type = "color", value = "#EAEAEA" },
  head = { type = "color", value = "#F7F7F7" },
  fg = { type = "color", value = "#111111" },
  muted = { type = "color", value = "#6E6E6E" },
})
G.columns = {
  { key = "name", title = "Customer", width = 220 },
  { key = "email", title = "Email", width = "fill_container" },
  { key = "status", title = "Status", width = 120 },
  { key = "joined", title = "Joined", width = 130 },
  { key = "total", title = "Total", width = 110, alignEnd = true },
}
G.rows = {
  { name = "Amara Okafor", email = "amara@lumenhq.com", status = "Active", joined = "Mar 3, 2025", total = "$12,480" },
  { name = "Jonas Lindqvist", email = "jonas.lindqvist@northpeak-logistics.se", status = "Trial", joined = "Apr 18, 2025", total = "$0" },
  { name = "Priya Raman", email = "priya@quill.dev", status = "Past due", joined = "Jan 9, 2024", total = "$3,215" },
  { name = "Mateo Ruiz", email = "mateo@ruiz.studio", status = "Active", joined = "Jun 27, 2025", total = "$880" },
}
```

```luau
local badgeColors = { Active = { "#DCFCE7", "#166534" }, Trial = { "#E0E7FF", "#3730A3" }, ["Past due"] = { "#FEE2E2", "#991B1B" } }
local function cell(column, child)
  return { type = "frame", name = `{column.title} cell`, width = column.width, height = "fill_container",
    layout = "horizontal", padding = { 0, 16 }, alignItems = "center", justifyContent = if column.alignEnd then "end" else "start", children = { child } }
end
local function text(content, header)
  return { type = "text", name = "Value", content = content, fontSize = if header then 12 else 14, fontWeight = if header then 600 else 400,
    fill = if header then "$muted" else "$fg" }
end
local function badge(status)
  return { type = "frame", name = "Badge", padding = { 2, 8 }, cornerRadius = 999, fill = badgeColors[status][1], children = {
    { type = "text", name = "Badge label", content = status, fontSize = 12, fontWeight = 500, fill = badgeColors[status][2] } } }
end
local function row(name, children, fill)
  return { type = "frame", name = name, width = "fill_container", height = 52, layout = "horizontal", fill = fill,
    stroke = { fill = "$line", thickness = { top = 0, right = 0, bottom = 1, left = 0 } }, children = children }
end
local headerCells = {}
for _, c in G.columns do
  table.insert(headerCells, cell(c, text(c.title, true)))
end
local rows = { row("Header", headerCells, "$head") }
for _, r in G.rows do
  local cells = {}
  for _, c in G.columns do
    table.insert(cells, cell(c, if c.key == "status" then badge(r.status) else text(r[c.key], false)))
  end
  table.insert(rows, row(r.name, cells, "$panel"))
end
G.table = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Customers table", x = 0, y = 0, width = 960, layout = "vertical", cornerRadius = 10, clip = true,
  fill = "$panel", stroke = { fill = "$line", thickness = 1 }, children = rows })
local clipped = {}
Get(G.table, function(node, info)
  if #info.problems > 0 then table.insert(clipped, `{node.name}: ` .. table.concat(info.problems, "; ")) end
end)
Print(if #clipped > 0 then clipped else "clean")
TakeScreenshot(G.table)
```

A cell neither wraps nor cuts off its text: a value longer than its column runs into the next one, and only the table's own `clip` reports it. Compare each text's bounds with its cell's in a visitor, then widen the column, shorten the value, or give the text `width = "fill_container"` so it wraps.

## Around the table

- **Toolbar** above: search on the left, filters beside it, the primary action on the right.
- **Footer** below: a count ("Showing 1–20 of 248") on the left, pagination on the right.
- Row actions sit in the last column as an icon button (`lucide:ellipsis`), or appear on the selected row.
- Selected rows get a quiet fill; selection checkboxes go in a narrow first column.
- Zebra stripes only for very wide or dense tables; borders between rows usually do the job.

```luau
local spot = FindEmptySpace({ width = 960, height = 36, from = G.table, direction = "top", padding = 16 })
local toolbar = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Toolbar", x = spot.x, y = spot.y, width = 960, layout = "horizontal", justifyContent = "space_between", alignItems = "center", children = {
  { type = "frame", name = "Search", width = 280, height = 36, layout = "horizontal", gap = 8, padding = { 0, 12 }, alignItems = "center", cornerRadius = 8,
    stroke = { fill = "$line", thickness = 1 }, children = {
      { type = "icon", name = "Search icon", icon = "lucide:search", width = 16, height = 16, fill = "$muted" },
      { type = "text", name = "Placeholder", content = "Search customers", fontSize = 14, fill = "$muted" },
    } },
  { type = "frame", name = "More", width = 36, height = 36, cornerRadius = 8, layout = "horizontal", justifyContent = "center", alignItems = "center",
    stroke = { fill = "$line", thickness = 1 }, children = { { type = "icon", name = "More icon", icon = "lucide:ellipsis", width = 16, height = 16, fill = "$fg" } } },
} })
TakeScreenshot(toolbar)
```

## Large tables

Build a long table across calls: the header and the first rows in one call, more rows in the next (`Insert(G.table, row(…))` with the helpers defined again, since functions don't carry between calls). A table in a Screen shows a page of rows (10–20) and the pagination, never hundreds.

On a phone, a table becomes a list of cards: the name as the title, two or three key fields below, the status as a badge.
]]

M.TOPICS["code"] = [[
# Code: implementing a Design

How a coding agent turns a Design into the project's code. You read the Design with snippets that only read and Print (they change nothing, so they leave no undo steps the person would see as changes), then write code in the project's own stack.

## Rules

- Use the project's framework, styling approach, component library and versions. Look at how the codebase does things before writing anything.
- Reuse first: extend an existing component rather than duplicating it, and keep its behaviour working.
- One component at a time: read its full tree, implement it, compare with a screenshot, then move to the next.
- Match exactly: the copy, icons (the same Iconify set and name, through the project's icon package where it has one), spacing, radii, font families, sizes and weights, colours.
- Colours and other Token values go through CSS custom properties named after the Tokens, never pasted hex.
- Path geometry is copied as SVG path data, not redrawn.
- Write code, not documents: no summary files the person didn't ask for.

The examples below read this small sample Design (a Button Component and a Screen using it); run them against yours by changing the ids.

```luau
SetThemes("mode", { "light", "dark" })
SetTokens({
  primary = { type = "color", value = { { value = "#4338CA", theme = { mode = "light" } }, { value = "#A5B4FC", theme = { mode = "dark" } } } },
  ["on-primary"] = { type = "color", value = { { value = "#FFFFFF", theme = { mode = "light" } }, { value = "#1E1B4B", theme = { mode = "dark" } } } },
  radius = { type = "number", value = 8 },
})
G.button = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Button", reusable = true, x = 0, y = 0, layout = "horizontal", gap = 6, padding = { 8, 14 }, cornerRadius = "$radius",
  fill = "$primary", alignItems = "center", children = { { type = "text", name = "Label", content = "Save", fontSize = 14, fontWeight = 600, fill = "$on-primary" } } })
G.screen = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Settings", x = 0, y = 120, width = 480, layout = "vertical", gap = 16, padding = 24, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
Copy(G.button, G.screen, {})
Copy(G.button, G.screen, { descendants = { Label = { content = "Save and close" } } })
```

## 1. Inventory

Start with what's there: the Screens, the Components, and how often each Component is used.

```luau
local uses = {}
local function count(node)
  if node.type == "instance" then uses[node.ref] = (uses[node.ref] or 0) + 1 end
  for _, child in node.children or {} do count(child) end
end
for _, node in Get() do count(Get(node.id, { depth = math.huge })) end
local screens, components = {}, {}
for _, node in Get() do
  if node.reusable then
    table.insert(components, `{node.id} {node.name} used {uses[node.id] or 0}x`)
  elseif node.type == "frame" then
    table.insert(screens, `{node.id} {node.name}`)
  end
end
Print("Screens:", screens)
Print("Components:", components)
```

Count the Instances again in your code when you're done: each should be a use of your component.

## 2. Tokens

`GetTokens()` gives the Tokens as the Design holds them. For CSS, every HTML `Export` (see *Exports*) begins its `<style>` with each Token as a CSS custom property (`--name`, any character other than a letter, digit, `-` or `_` becoming `-`; numbers without units): the default Theme's values on `:root` and each other Theme's under a data attribute (`[data-mode="dark"]`). With `css = "tailwind"` and `scaffold = false`, that block is the whole `<style>`. Put it into the project's global stylesheet (or map it onto the project's existing variables).

```luau
Print(GetTokens().tokens)
Export({ G.button }, { format = "html", css = "tailwind", scaffold = false })
```

## 3. Each component

Read the Component whole with Tokens resolved, so you see the values you'll write; read it again without resolving to see which Tokens each property uses (the custom property names you'll write).

```luau
Print(Get(G.button, { depth = math.huge, resolveTokens = true }))
Print(Get(G.button, { depth = math.huge }))
```

Then its Instances: each Instance's overrides (`descendants`, and its own properties) are its props. A property overridden somewhere becomes a prop; a node some Instance hides (`enabled = false`) becomes an optional part.

```luau
local function props(node)
  if node.type == "instance" then
    local own = table.clone(node)
    own.id, own.ref, own.descendants, own.type = nil, nil, nil, nil
    Print(node.id, "of", node.ref, { own = own, descendants = node.descendants })
  end
  for _, child in node.children or {} do props(child) end
end
props(Get(G.screen, { depth = math.huge }))
Print(Get(G.screen, { depth = math.huge, expandInstances = true, resolveTokens = true }))
```

How the model maps to CSS:

- `layout = "horizontal" | "vertical"` is `display: flex` with `flex-direction: row | column`; `gap` and `padding` as written; `justifyContent` and `alignItems` as their CSS namesakes (`space_between` is `space-between`).
- `fill_container` is `flex: 1 1 0` along the parent's direction and `align-self: stretch` across it; `fit_content` is the element's natural size; a number is a fixed size.
- A child in a Frame without a layout, or one marked `absolute`, is `position: absolute` at its `x` and `y`.
- A `stroke` takes no room from the layout, unlike a CSS border: draw it as a box shadow (`inset 0 0 0 1px` for `inside`, a spread for `outside`) or an outline.
- `lineHeight` is a unitless line height; `letterSpacing` is in px.
- A shadow's `blur` is CSS's blur value; a `blur` or `background_blur` effect's `radius` is twice CSS's (`backdrop-filter: blur(radius / 2)`).
- An icon is the Iconify icon `set:name` at the node's size, in its fill colour.

## 4. Compare

Screenshot what you're implementing, with the node as small as shows it, and compare with your page. Check the behaviour the Design implies: fill grows with its container, fit shrinks to its content, nothing overflows sideways at the design width, the console is clean.

```luau
TakeScreenshot({ G.button, G.screen })
```

## Exports

`Export` writes files and returns their paths (`exported.files`, `exported.folder`): by default into your chat's folder under `design-exports/<Design name>/`; `folder = "<whole path in the project>"` puts them where the project keeps such things (e.g. `public/images`). Read the files from there. On a Zeron that can't write files yet, they come back in the result instead.

- Everything at once: `Export({ format = "handoff" })` writes each Screen as a 2x PNG, `index.html` and `tokens.json`. Past about 20 s of drawing it stops and `exported.next` is the call for the rest.
- PNG of any node, for assets or comparison: `Export({ id }, { format = "png", scale = 2 })` writes it as a file (`inline = true` also attaches it to the result, like a screenshot).
- HTML of Screens, with plain CSS or Tailwind: `Export({ screens }, { format = "html", css = "css" })` writes the page as a `.html` file (`inline = true` also returns it under `exported.html`). Auto layout becomes flexbox, Tokens become custom properties, shapes and icons inline SVG. `scaffold = false` leaves out the document around it (doctype, head, fonts); `layerNames = true` keeps each node's name as a `data-name` attribute. It's a reference for structure and values, not code to ship: rebuild it in the project's components.

```luau
Export({ G.screen }, { format = "html", css = "css", layerNames = true })
Export({ G.button }, { format = "png", scale = 2 })
```
]]

M.TOPICS["tailwind"] = [[
# Tailwind

Implementing a Design with Tailwind CSS (v4). Read the `code` topic guide first for how to read the Design; this guide is how to write what you read as Tailwind.

## Setup

- One `@import "tailwindcss";` at the top of the main stylesheet; no hand-written resets (Tailwind's preflight is one).
- The Design's Tokens go in as CSS custom properties: a Tailwind HTML `Export` (`css = "tailwind"`) gives them as the whole of its `<style>`, the default Theme on `:root` and the others under data attributes (`[data-mode="dark"]`). Set that attribute on the element (often `<html>`) to switch Themes.
- Fonts load with a `<link>` to Google Fonts or the framework's font loader, not `@import url()` inside the CSS (it must come first and blocks rendering).
- A font stack containing commas doesn't survive being passed through a variable into an arbitrary value. Give each family a class of its own in `@layer base` or `@theme` (`--font-display: "Instrument Serif", serif;` then `font-display`), or with a framework font loader use the variable it gives you directly.
- `html`, `body` and the app root are `h-full`; the root of each Screen is `h-full w-full`. The Design's Screen width is the layout it was designed at, not a width to fix: only things that really are fixed (a sidebar, an icon button) keep their pixel width.

```luau
SetThemes("mode", { "light", "dark" })
SetTokens({
  surface = { type = "color", value = { { value = "#FFFFFF", theme = { mode = "light" } }, { value = "#0B0B0F", theme = { mode = "dark" } } } },
  text = { type = "color", value = { { value = "#0B0B0F", theme = { mode = "light" } }, { value = "#F4F4F5", theme = { mode = "dark" } } } },
  brand = { type = "color", value = "#7C3AED" },
  radius = { type = "number", value = 12 },
})
Print(GetTokens().tokens)
```

## Styling

- Utilities only, no inline `style`. Arbitrary values hit the Design's numbers exactly: `w-[280px]`, `gap-[18px]`, `text-[15px]`, `leading-[1.45]`, `tracking-[-0.5px]`.
- Colours through the Token variables, never hex: `bg-[var(--brand)]`, `text-[var(--text)]`, `border-[var(--border)]`. In v4 the shorthand `bg-(--brand)` works too.
- Radius and other number Tokens the same way: `rounded-[calc(var(--radius)*1px)]`, or define them with units in your own `@theme`.
- States with variants: `hover:`, `focus-visible:`, `active:`, `disabled:`, `aria-selected:`, and `dark:` only if the project's dark mode uses a class; with Token Themes, the variables switch on their own.
- SVG icons and paths: `fill-[var(--x)]`, `stroke-[var(--x)]`, sized with `size-4` or `size-[18px]`.

## Layout

| Design | Tailwind |
| --- | --- |
| `layout = "horizontal"` / `"vertical"` | `flex flex-row` / `flex flex-col` |
| `gap = 12`, `padding = { 8, 16 }` | `gap-[12px] py-[8px] px-[16px]` |
| `justifyContent = "space_between"` | `justify-between` |
| `alignItems = "center"` | `items-center` |
| `width = "fill_container"` in a row | `flex-1 min-w-0` |
| `width = "fill_container"` in a column | `self-stretch` (or `w-full`) |
| `height = "fill_container"` in a column | `flex-1 min-h-0`, with every parent up the chain given a height |
| `fit_content` | `w-fit` / `h-fit`, or nothing |
| `absolute = true`, `x`, `y` | `absolute left-[x] top-[y]` in a `relative` parent |
| `clip = true` | `overflow-hidden` |

A region that scrolls in the real app (a list in a fixed-height panel) is `flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-auto`; the Design shows it unscrolled.

## Starting from an export

`Export({ screen }, { format = "html", css = "tailwind" })` writes a Screen as Tailwind-classed HTML to a file (its path in `exported.files`; `inline = true` also returns it under `exported.html`): exact values as arbitrary classes, Token colours as `var()` references. Use it to read off structure and values, then rebuild it in the project's components; flatten the wrappers it doesn't need.

```luau
local card = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Plan card", x = 0, y = 0, width = 320, layout = "vertical", gap = 16, padding = 24,
  cornerRadius = "$radius", fill = "$surface", stroke = { fill = "#E4E4E7", thickness = 1 }, children = {
    { type = "text", name = "Plan", content = "Team", fontSize = 15, fontWeight = 600, fill = "$text" },
    { type = "text", name = "Price", content = "$24 per seat", fontSize = 28, fontWeight = 700, letterSpacing = -0.5, fill = "$text" },
    { type = "frame", name = "Choose", width = "fill_container", layout = "horizontal", justifyContent = "center", padding = { 10, 16 }, cornerRadius = 999, fill = "$brand",
      children = { { type = "text", name = "Choose label", content = "Choose Team", fontSize = 14, fontWeight = 600, fill = "#FFFFFF" } } },
  } })
Export({ card }, { format = "html", css = "tailwind", scaffold = false })
```

Written by hand in the project, the same card is:

```html
<div class="flex w-[320px] flex-col gap-[16px] rounded-[12px] bg-[var(--surface)] p-[24px] shadow-[inset_0_0_0_1px_#E4E4E7]">
  <p class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">Team</p>
  <p class="text-[28px] font-bold tracking-[-0.5px] text-[var(--text)]">$24 per seat</p>
  <button class="flex justify-center self-stretch rounded-full bg-[var(--brand)] px-[16px] py-[10px] text-[14px] font-semibold text-white">Choose Team</button>
</div>
```
]]

M.TOPICS["prototyping"] = [[
# Prototyping: states, links and flows

Make Screens clickable only when the person asks for a prototype, or for something "clickable" or "interactive". A prototype is the same Design with three additions:

- **states:** how a layer looks while it's hovered or pressed;
- **interactions:** where a click takes you, and with which transition;
- **flows:** the starting points.

and, for a Screen that scrolls, its device, scrolling frames, and fixed and sticky layers (see Scrolling). Interactive components (a switch, tabs) and **variables** (a cart count, signed in, dark mode, a score) build on these.

The person plays it in the Design tab with **Play** (Ctrl+Enter). You play it yourself with **`Simulate`** (see Simulate): click through it, read what's shown, take screenshots, and fix what it finds before you hand it over.

## States: hover and press

Put `states` on the layer people point at: the button or the card, not its text.

```luau
G.home = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Home", x = 0, y = 0, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
G.button = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Button", reusable = true, x = 0, y = -160, layout = "horizontal", justifyContent = "center", padding = { 14, 24 }, width = 326, fill = "#4F46E5", cornerRadius = 12,
  states = {
    hover = { fill = "#4338CA" },
    press = { fill = "#3730A3", scale = 0.97 },
    transition = { duration = 150, easing = "ease-out" },
  },
  children = { { type = "text", name = "Label", content = "Continue", fontSize = 16, fontWeight = 600, fill = "#FFFFFF" } } })
G.cta = Copy(G.button, G.home, { name = "Get started", descendants = { Label = { content = "Get started" } } })
```

- **What a state may set:** `fill`, `stroke`, `effect`, `opacity`, `cornerRadius` and `scale` (0.5 to 2, about the centre). Tokens work (`"$primary-hover"`).
- **What it may not set:** anything that changes layout, such as width, height, padding, gap, text content or font size. These are refused with an error. Use a separate Screen instead.
- **Press** applies on top of hover.
- **Transition:** it defaults to 150 ms ease-out. Use `duration = 0` to snap.
- **Good defaults:** hover is one step darker (or lighter on dark); press is darker still, plus `scale = 0.97`.
- **Children:** to recolour a layer inside when the parent is hovered, use `descendants` inside the state. Name the layer by its id or by a name that's unique inside, e.g. `hover = { fill = "#EEF2FF", descendants = { Title = { fill = "#4F46E5" } } }`.
- **Components:** put states on the Component, and every Instance hovers alike. An Instance's own `states` replaces the Component's entirely. `Update(inst, { states = null })` brings the Component's back.

## Links

`Link(layer, to, transition)` adds a click link. `to` is a Screen's id, `"back"`, or a URL.

```luau
G.next = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Next", x = 490, y = 0, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
G.back = Insert(G.next, { type = "text", name = "Back", content = "← Back", fontSize = 15, fill = "#0F172A", states = { hover = { opacity = 0.6 } } })
Link(G.cta, G.next, "push-left")
Link(G.back, "back")
Link(Insert(G.next, { type = "text", name = "Terms", content = "Terms", fontSize = 14, fill = "#475569" }), "https://example.com/terms")
```

- **Transitions as short strings:**
  - `"instant"` and `"dissolve"`;
  - `"push-<dir>"`, `"slide-in-<dir>"` (or `"slide-<dir>"`), `"slide-out-<dir>"`, `"move-in-<dir>"` and `"move-out-<dir>"`.
  
  `<dir>` is the direction the motion travels: `left`, `right`, `up` or `down`. They store 300 ms, ease-out.
- **Choosing one:**
  - forward in a flow: `push-left`;
  - tabs and same-level switches: `dissolve`;
  - a sheet that rises: `slide-up`;
  - back buttons: just `"back"`, which plays the reverse of the way in.
  
  Keep durations at 200–400 ms.
- **Full table:** `Link(a, b, { type = "dissolve", duration = 400, easing = "ease-in-out" })`. Easings are `linear`, `ease-in`, `ease-out`, `ease-in-out`, `ease-in-back`, `ease-out-back`, `ease-in-out-back`, or `{ x1, y1, x2, y2 }`.
- **Multiple links:** `Link` appends a click reaction. `{ replace = true }` as the fourth argument replaces the layer's click links. On an Instance, its own link replaces the one it inherits.
- **Rules:**
  - Only Screens are targets (top-level frames, not Components).
  - Link the outermost clickable layer, not its text.
  - Don't put a link inside another link.

## Triggers and lists of actions

A reaction starts on a **trigger** and runs its **actions in order**, each after the one before it has finished animating (Figma's rule). `Link`'s fourth argument picks the trigger; `append = true` adds the action to the end of that trigger's list instead of making a new reaction.

```luau
G.splash = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Splash", x = 0, y = 1000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#4F46E5" })
G.menu = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Menu", x = 490, y = 1000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC" })
G.menuButton = Insert(G.home, { type = "frame", name = "Menu button", width = 120, height = 44, cornerRadius = 10, fill = "#E2E8F0" })
G.card = Insert(G.home, { type = "frame", name = "Card", width = 326, height = 120, cornerRadius = 16, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
Link(G.splash, G.home, "dissolve", { trigger = { type = "afterDelay", ms = 1500 }, easing = "gentle" })
Link(G.menuButton, G.menu, "dissolve", { trigger = "hover" })   -- while hovering; back when the pointer leaves
Link(G.home, G.next, "slide-up", { trigger = { type = "key", keys = { "Ctrl", "K" } } })
Link(G.card, G.next, "push-left", { trigger = "drag", easing = "quick" })
Link(G.card, G.next, "dissolve")
Link(G.card, G.menu, "push-left", { append = true, easing = "bouncy" })   -- then on to Menu
```

- **Triggers** (`trigger =` a name, or a table with its settings):
  - `"click"` (the default);
  - `"hover"` and `"press"`: the actions run while the pointer is over the layer, or while it's held, and **revert** when that ends (a Screen they went to goes back the reverse way): menus, tooltips, peeks;
  - `"mouseEnter"`, `"mouseLeave"`, `"mouseDown"`, `"mouseUp"`: one-off, with no revert. `{ type = "mouseEnter", delay = 300 }` waits until the pointer has stayed 300 ms. Pair enter and leave to undo something by hand;
  - `{ type = "afterDelay", ms = 1500 }`: once, that long after its Screen shows. Splash Screens, auto-advancing steps. Put it on the Screen itself;
  - `{ type = "key", keys = "Enter" }` or `keys = { "Shift", "D" }`: one key (a letter, a digit, `Enter`, `Escape`, `Space`, `Tab`, `Backspace`, `Delete`, the arrows `ArrowLeft`…, `Home`, `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`, `F1`–`F12`) with any of `Shift`, `Ctrl`, `Alt`, `Meta`. It works while its Screen shows, and wins over Play's own keys (Esc and Ctrl+Enter still close);
  - `"drag"`: a press that moves 4 points runs it (a swipe to the next card).
- **One of each trigger per layer**, except `key` and `drag` (`duplicate-trigger` warns; only the first runs). Don't put `click` and `hover` on one layer: use `mouseEnter` and `mouseLeave` with the click.
- **Several actions:** `actions = { a, b, … }` all run, in order. A navigate in the middle carries on from the Screen it reached.
- **Reserved:** `{ action = "motion", play = id }` (play a timeline motion) is kept in the file but doesn't play yet (`motion-reserved` warns).

## Easing: springs and curves

- **Durations** go up to 10,000 ms.
- **Springs** feel physical and natural for sheets, cards and anything dragged. A spring sets its own duration (when it comes to rest; `"quick"` 708 ms, `"gentle"` 792 ms), as in Figma, so don't pass a `duration` with one: it's ignored and `spring-duration` warns. For a set time, use a curve such as `"ease-out"`:
  - presets `"gentle"` (soft, the most neutral), `"quick"` (fast, short settle: toasts), `"bouncy"` (overshoots and wobbles: playful moments), `"slow"` (steady, no overshoot: big content);
  - `{ spring = { mass = 1, stiffness = 170, damping = 26, velocity = 0 } }`: more stiffness is faster, more damping is less bounce, more mass is heavier. A spring that takes over 10 s to settle is refused.
- **Custom bézier:** `{ x1, y1, x2, y2 }`, x within 0–1 (as CSS `cubic-bezier`).
- `Link(a, b, "push-left", { easing = "bouncy" })`, or in the full table: `transition = { type = "push", direction = "left", easing = "bouncy" }`. State transitions take springs too: `states = { hover = { scale = 1.03 }, transition = { easing = "quick" } }`.
- The HTML export plays springs as CSS `linear()`, the same curve as Play.

## Flows

The first flow is where Play starts.

```luau
SetFlows({ { name = "Onboarding", start = G.home, description = "New user signs up.\n- Welcome\n- Sign up\nSpec: https://example.com/spec" } })
Print(GetFlows()[1].name)
```

- `description` (optional, plain text, at most 2,000 characters) shows under the flows in Play's **Flows** sidebar. Lines starting `- ` or `1. ` show as lists, and `https://…` as links.
- The sidebar opens by itself when there are 2+ flows. Picking a flow there restarts at its start, as Restart does.

## Scrolling and the device

Play shows each Screen in a **device**: a Screen taller than the device scrolls inside it, as on a phone. Inside a Screen, a frame with `overflow` scrolls its own content (a list, a carousel), and a layer with `scroll` stays put (`"fixed"`: a header, a tab bar) or sticks at the top (`"sticky"`: a section title).

```luau
SetPrototype({ device = "iphone-16" })
G.feed = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Feed", x = 980, y = 0, width = 393, height = 1400, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", gap = 16, padding = { 80, 16, 100, 16 } })
Insert(G.feed, { type = "frame", name = "Header", absolute = true, x = 0, y = 0, width = 393, height = 64, fill = "#0F172A", scroll = "fixed" })
G.stories = Insert(G.feed, { type = "frame", name = "Stories", width = 361, height = 120, overflow = "horizontal", layout = "horizontal", gap = 12 })
for i = 1, 6 do Insert(G.stories, { type = "frame", name = "Story", width = 96, height = 120, cornerRadius = 16, fill = "#C7D2FE" }) end
G.section = Insert(G.feed, { type = "frame", name = "Recent", width = 361, layout = "vertical", gap = 12 })
Insert(G.section, { type = "text", name = "Recent title", content = "Recent", fontSize = 15, fontWeight = 600, fill = "#0F172A", scroll = "sticky" })
for i = 1, 8 do Insert(G.section, { type = "frame", name = "Row", width = 361, height = 72, cornerRadius = 12, fill = "#FFFFFF" }) end
G.tabbar = Insert(G.feed, { type = "frame", name = "Tab bar", absolute = true, x = 0, y = 852 - 84, width = 393, height = 84, fill = "#FFFFFF", scroll = "fixed" })
G.toTop = Insert(G.tabbar, { type = "frame", name = "Top", x = 24, y = 16, width = 64, height = 40, cornerRadius = 10, fill = "#4F46E5",
  interactions = { { trigger = "click", actions = { { action = "scrollTo", to = G.section, offset = 72, transition = { type = "animate", duration = 400, easing = "ease-in-out" } } } } } })
```

- **The device:** `SetPrototype({ device = "iphone-16" })` sets Play's viewport for every Screen, and draws the device round it. See Presenting for the presets and the other keys. Without a device, each Screen plays at its own size.
- **Make the Screen as tall as its content** and let it scroll: a phone feed is 393 wide and as tall as it needs. Put what must stay visible (header, tab bar) in `scroll = "fixed"` layers, with `absolute = true` at their place in the device (a tab bar at `y = 852 - 84` on an iPhone 16).
- **`overflow = "vertical" | "horizontal" | "both"`** on a frame with a **fixed size**: its content scrolls inside it. A frame that hugs its content has nothing to scroll (`scroll-fits` warns): give it a width or height, or wrap the content in a frame that scrolls. Horizontal is the carousel.
- **`scroll = "fixed"`** works on a Screen's own layers and on a scrolling frame's own layers. In an auto layout, also set `absolute = true` (`scroll-fixed` warns otherwise).
- **`scroll = "sticky"`** scrolls with the rest, then sticks at the top of what scrolls while its own parent is still in view: section titles inside their section.
- **Scroll to:** `{ action = "scrollTo", to = layerId, offset?, transition? }` scrolls the nearest scrolling frame around the layer (or the Screen) so the layer is at its top, less `offset` points. `transition = { type = "animate", duration, easing }`, or none for a jump. The layer must be on the same Screen (`scroll-target` warns).
- **Scroll memory:** as in Figma, a Screen opens scrolled where it was last, shared with Screens of the same name, or the same name before a "/" (`Checkout / 1`, `Checkout / 2`). `{ action = "navigate", to = id, resetScroll = true }` opens it at the top.
- In Play the wheel scrolls the innermost scrolling frame under the pointer, a drag scrolls (with momentum), and Restart forgets the positions. The HTML export scrolls with real CSS (`overflow`, `position: sticky`).

## Presenting

```luau
SetPrototype({ device = "iphone-16", model = "Pink", rotate = false, fit = "fit", background = "#111827" })
SetPrototype({ showFrame = false })
Print(GetPrototype().model)
```

- **`device`:** a preset, or `{ w = 390, h = 844 }` (no frame drawn).
  - Phones: `iphone-17`, `iphone-17-pro`, `iphone-17-pro-max`, `iphone-air`, `iphone-16`, `iphone-16-pro`, `iphone-16-pro-max`, `iphone-se`, `android-compact`, `android-medium`.
  - Tablets: `ipad-mini`, `ipad-pro-11`, `ipad-pro-12-9`, `ipad-pro-13`, `android-expanded`, `surface-pro-8`.
  - Watches: `apple-watch-41`, `apple-watch` (45 mm).
  - Computers and TV: `macbook-air`, `macbook-pro-14`, `macbook-pro-16`, `desktop`, `tv`.
- **`model`:** the frame's colour, one of the preset's (`iphone-16`: Black, White, Pink, Teal, Ultramarine). A wrong name fails with the list. Changing the device drops a model it doesn't have.
- **`rotate = true`:** landscape. The device turns, but the Screen keeps its layout (a portrait Screen sits at the top left and scrolls), as Figma's Fixed size.
- **`showFrame = false`:** no frame drawn (the default is to draw it for a preset).
- **`fit`:** how Play scales into the window:
  - `"fit"` (the default): the whole Screen, at most 100%;
  - `"width"`: fit the width (a long Screen scrolls);
  - `"actual"`: 100%;
  - `"fill"`: cover the window, cropped;
  - `"responsive"`: see below.
- **`deviceFit`** (with a frame): `"fit"` (the device fits on screen), `"fill"` (zoom the device to fill the screen), `"actual"` (100%).
- **`background`:** the colour round the device.
- `SetPrototype` changes only the keys you give. `SetPrototype(nil)` clears them all.
- **Play's own menus** (Options, and the device name in the bar) change the scaling for that session only. Z cycles it. The Design keeps your keys.
- **Responsive** lays each Screen out again at the window's width (with a device, at the device's size, so landscape re-lays out). Auto layout, fill and hug reflow. The Screen is as tall as the window, or as its content if that's taller (then it scrolls). **There are no constraints:** a layer outside auto layout keeps its left/top, so pin things with auto layout (fill, hug, padding) if they must stretch or follow the right edge. Overlays keep their size.
- The HTML export draws the same frame, scaled to fit the browser window. It hides the frame when the window is under 1.2× the device's width, or with `?frame=0` in the URL.
- **Play's keys:** → (or Space, N) goes forward: forward history, else the shown Screen's first link to a Screen not yet visited, else the next Screen by x, then y. ← is Back, R restarts, Z cycles the scaling, Ctrl+\ hides the bar. A click on nothing flashes the hotspots (Options › Show hints on click). A key trigger of the Design's own wins over these.
- **The person, in the editor** (not for snippets; say them if asked): Shift+Space opens a small live Preview beside the canvas (it follows edits and jumps to a Screen clicked on the canvas); Shift+E switches the right panel between Design and Prototype; on the Prototype tab a noodle can be clicked, its arrow end dragged to another Screen, or dragged off to remove it; Ctrl+C / X / V copy, cut and append interactions; with several layers selected the panel edits them all (differing values read "Mixed"); "Select N matching interactions" picks the layers with the same name and link in other Screens.

## Simulate: play it yourself

`Simulate(start, steps, opts?)` plays the prototype headless, by Play's own rules:
- the same triggers, action lists, transition times, history and Back;
- scroll memory, overlays and hover/press reverts.

It returns what a person would see after each step, and the problems it met. Run it after every prototype change, the way you'd click through it.

**Worked example.** Build a 3-Screen sign-up, then Simulate it and fix what it finds:

```luau
G.suWelcome = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Sign up / Welcome", x = 0, y = 2000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#FFFFFF", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
G.suForm = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Sign up / Details", x = 440, y = 2000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#FFFFFF", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
G.suDone = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Sign up / Done", x = 880, y = 2000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#FFFFFF", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
Insert(G.suWelcome, { type = "text", name = "Title", content = "Create your account", fontSize = 28, fontWeight = 700, fill = "#0F172A" })
G.suStart = Insert(G.suWelcome, { type = "frame", name = "Start", width = 326, height = 52, cornerRadius = 12, fill = "#4F46E5", states = { hover = { fill = "#4338CA" } } })
Insert(G.suForm, { type = "frame", name = "Email", width = 326, height = 52, cornerRadius = 12, stroke = { fill = "#CBD5E1", thickness = 1 } })
G.suNext = Insert(G.suForm, { type = "frame", name = "Create account", width = 326, height = 52, cornerRadius = 12, fill = "#4F46E5" })
G.suBack = Insert(G.suForm, { type = "text", name = "Back", content = "← Back", fontSize = 15, fill = "#0F172A" })
Insert(G.suDone, { type = "text", name = "Done title", content = "You're in", fontSize = 28, fontWeight = 700, fill = "#0F172A" })
Link(G.suStart, G.suForm, "push-left")
Link(G.suBack, "back")
```

```luau
local run = Simulate(G.suWelcome, { "click Start", "click Create account" })
Print(run.screen.name)          --> Sign up / Details
for _, p in run.problems or {} do Print(p.kind, p.message) end
--> dead-click   clicking "Create account" on Sign up / Details does nothing: no click reaction on it or around it
--> unreachable  1 Screen can't be reached from "Sign up / Welcome" (…): "Sign up / Done" (…) …
```

Fix what it found, then play it again, with a screenshot halfway through the last push and one at the end:

```luau
Link(G.suNext, G.suDone, "push-left")
Link(G.suDone, G.suWelcome, "dissolve", { trigger = { type = "afterDelay", ms = 2000 } })
local run = Simulate(G.suWelcome, { "click Start", "back", "click Start", { click = "Create account", shot = 150 }, "wait 2500" }, { shots = "end" })
Print(run.screen.name, #run.shots)  --> Sign up / Welcome 2
for _, p in run.problems or {} do Print(p.kind, p.message) end   --> (none about the sign-up)
```

- **Start:** a Screen id, a Screen's name, or a flow's name. Without one, it starts at the first flow, or at the first Screen.
- **Steps:**
  - `"click <layer>"`, `"hover <layer>"`, `"leave"`, `"press <layer>"`, `"release"`;
  - `"drag <layer>"` (runs its drag trigger), or `"drag <layer> 0 -300"` (scrolls, as a finger does, when nothing there has a drag trigger). On a drag trigger that smart-animates (navigate or Change to) a layer that travels, as a switch's knob, `"drag Knob 12 0"` scrubs as in Play: the transition follows the drag along the knob's travel, and on release lands forward past half way, else goes back. The step's `scrub` says `p` (0 to 1), `lands` and `ms`; `{ drag = "Knob", by = { x = 12, y = 0 }, shot = 0 }` is the frame where it was let go. A layer that doesn't travel (the switch's track) plays the transition through. Under 4 points isn't a drag;
  - `"key Enter"`, `"key Ctrl+K"`;
  - `"wait 1500"` (timers run: afterDelay, delayed mouseEnter);
  - `"scroll 400"`, or `"scroll <layer> 300 0"` (a wheel over that layer);
  - `"back"` and `"restart"` (Play's ← and R);
  - `"shot"`.
  
  A `<layer>` is an id or a name path from the Screen (`"Form/Continue"`, or just `"Continue"` when that's unique). Layers on an open overlay come first.
- **Clicks act like a pointer.** A click points at the middle of what shows of the layer, as a person would, so it also hovers and presses. It goes to whatever is on top there.
- **Waiting:** a step waits for a transition still running, then lets the transition it started finish. Time is virtual: a run is instant and gives the same result every time.
- **The result:**
  - `screen = { id, name }`, `overlays = { { id, name, x, y, w, h } }`, and `history` (how many Back steps there are);
  - `scroll`: scrollers that can move, with `layer`, `x`, `y` and `max`;
  - `states`: a layer hovered or pressed;
  - `pending`: timers still to run, with `in_ms` and `what`;
  - `opened`: URLs it would open;
  - `t`: ms since the start;
  - `steps`: per step, `did` (what ran: "navigate → Done (push left, 300 ms)"), `screen`, `overlays`, `notes`, `problems` and `error`. A step that can't run, like an unknown layer, has an `error`, and the rest still run;
  - `problems` and `ok`.
- **Problems** (`{ kind, message, layer?, screen? }`). Found on the way:
  - `dead-click`: nothing reacts there;
  - `covered`: another layer is on top of the one you meant, so the click went to it;
  - `offscreen`: scroll first;
  - `no-op`: a reaction ran but nothing changed, such as a link to the Screen you're on;
  - `no-reaction`: a key or drag nothing listens to;
  - `broken-link`, `back-empty`, `not-found`, `runaway` (timers that loop with no delay).
  
  Found over everything reachable from the start:
  - `unreachable` Screens;
  - `dead-end`: a Screen with no way on, which is fine for the last one;
  - `stuck-overlay`: an overlay nothing closes;
  - `covered` and `hidden-link`: links under another layer, cut off, or with no size;
  - `key-reserved`: Escape, which Play keeps for itself.
  
  Pass `{ check = false }` to get only what the steps met.
- **Screenshots:**
  - a `"shot"` step;
  - `{ click = "Next", shot = 150 }`, a frame 150 ms into what that step started (mid-transition);
  - `{ shots = "each" }` (after every step) or `"end"`.
  
  They come back with the call's other images, in order. `run.shots` says which step each one is. Each is the device viewport as Play shows it: scrolled, hovered, overlays and backdrop included. At most 12 a call.
- **The viewport** is the device (`SetPrototype`). Without one, it's each Screen's own size.

## Interactive components

A switch, a checkbox, tabs or an accordion is a **variant set**: Components with the same set name, each with its own property values (`On = "true"`). A link between two members is a **Change to**: an Instance that shows one member switches to the other, in place, without leaving the Screen. Every Instance of the set works this way, wherever it is used.

- `MakeVariants(set, { { props = { On = false }, node = id }, … })` makes the members (it returns their ids). Values are stored as text: `false` becomes `"false"`.
- `Variant(set, props)` returns a member's id. `Update(instance, { props = { On = "true" } })` shows another member on an Instance (the one whose props match best); its overrides carry over by layer name.
- `Link(layer, { On = "true" }, "smart")`, or `Link(layer, memberId, …)`, on a layer of a member, makes a Change to. It switches the closest Instance around that layer whose set holds the target, so a layer inside a nested Instance (a chevron in a row) changes its parent.
- Transitions: `instant`, `dissolve` or `smart`. With `smart`, the layers both members have tween (the knob slides, the colour fades). A member of another size reflows the Screen: an open accordion row pushes the layers below it down, on the same curve.
- Hover and press looks can be variants too: `Link(button, { State = "Hover" }, "instant", { trigger = "hover" })` goes back when the pointer leaves. For a plain look, `states` are simpler.
- **Memory (as Figma):** the member an Instance shows is remembered by its set and name path and shared everywhere, on every Screen and overlay. Turn "Wifi" on and every Instance named "Wifi" of that set shows on, on other Screens too, after Back, and in an overlay; an overlay's change shows on the Screen under it once it closes. A navigate with `resetComponents = true` forgets the target Screen's Instances; Restart forgets everything. So give Instances that mean different things different names.
- `Simulate` returns `components = { { layer = "Preferences/Wifi", set = "Switch", variant = "On=true", id } }`. Its steps say `change "Wifi" to …`, and its problems include `variant-target` (no Instance of the set around the layer) and `no-op` (it shows that member already). Lint warns about `variant-duplicate` (two members with the same props), `variant-props` (a member with other property names) and `variant-target`.
- The HTML export plays them too: each Screen's reachable variant states are sections of their own (at most 32 a Screen).

**A switch.** Two members; each changes to the other. Then a Screen with two of them, played:

```luau
G.swOff = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Switch / Off", x = 0, y = 4000, width = 52, height = 32, cornerRadius = 16, fill = "#CBD5E1" })
Insert(G.swOff, { type = "frame", name = "Knob", x = 4, y = 4, width = 24, height = 24, cornerRadius = 12, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
G.swOn = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Switch / On", x = 80, y = 4000, width = 52, height = 32, cornerRadius = 16, fill = "#16A34A" })
Insert(G.swOn, { type = "frame", name = "Knob", x = 24, y = 4, width = 24, height = 24, cornerRadius = 12, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
MakeVariants("Switch", { { props = { On = false }, node = G.swOff }, { props = { On = true }, node = G.swOn } })
Link(G.swOff, { On = "true" }, "smart", { easing = "quick" })
Link(G.swOn, { On = "false" }, "smart", { easing = "quick" })

G.prefs = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Preferences", x = 0, y = 4400, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", padding = 24, gap = 16 })
Insert(G.prefs, { type = "instance", ref = G.swOff, name = "Wifi" })
Insert(G.prefs, { type = "instance", ref = G.swOff, name = "Bluetooth" })
local run = Simulate(G.prefs, { "click Wifi" })
local shows = {}
for _, c in run.components or {} do shows[c.layer] = c.variant end
assert(shows["Preferences/Wifi"] == "On=true" and shows["Preferences/Bluetooth"] == "On=false", "the switch")
```

**A checkbox.** The tick only the checked member has fades in:

```luau
G.cbOff = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Checkbox / Off", x = 200, y = 4000, width = 24, height = 24, cornerRadius = 6, fill = "#FFFFFF", stroke = { fill = "#94A3B8", thickness = 2 } })
G.cbOn = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Checkbox / On", x = 240, y = 4000, width = 24, height = 24, cornerRadius = 6, fill = "#4F46E5" })
Insert(G.cbOn, { type = "text", name = "Tick", content = "✓", x = 6, y = 2, fontSize = 15, fontWeight = 700, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
MakeVariants("Checkbox", { { props = { Checked = false }, node = G.cbOff }, { props = { Checked = true }, node = G.cbOn } })
Link(G.cbOff, { Checked = "true" }, "dissolve", { duration = 150 })
Link(G.cbOn, { Checked = "false" }, "dissolve", { duration = 150 })

Insert(G.prefs, { type = "instance", ref = G.cbOff, name = "Terms" })
local run = Simulate(G.prefs, { "click Terms", "click Terms", "click Terms" })
local shows = {}
for _, c in run.components or {} do shows[c.layer] = c.variant end
assert(shows["Preferences/Terms"] == "Checked=true", "the checkbox")
```

**Tabs.** One member per tab; in each, the other tabs' labels change to their member, and the indicator slides:

```luau
local tabs = { "Home", "Search", "Profile" }
local members, labels = {}, {}
for i, active in tabs do
  local m = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Tabs / " .. active, x = 400, y = 3940 + i * 60, width = 390, height = 48, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
  labels[i] = {}
  for j, name in tabs do
    labels[i][j] = Insert(m, { type = "text", name = name, content = name, x = (j - 1) * 130 + 40, y = 14, fontSize = 15, fontWeight = if i == j then 600 else 400, fill = if i == j then "#4F46E5" else "#64748B" })
  end
  Insert(m, { type = "frame", name = "Indicator", x = (i - 1) * 130 + 15, y = 44, width = 100, height = 4, cornerRadius = 2, fill = "#4F46E5" })
  members[i] = { props = { Tab = active }, node = m }
end
G.tabs = MakeVariants("Tabs", members)
for i in tabs do
  for j, name in tabs do
    if i ~= j then Link(labels[i][j], { Tab = name }, "smart", { easing = "gentle" }) end
  end
end
```

**A segmented control.** The same shape, with a thumb that slides under the labels:

```luau
local ranges = { "Day", "Week", "Month" }
local members, labels = {}, {}
for i, active in ranges do
  local m = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Range / " .. active, x = 800, y = 3940 + i * 60, width = 300, height = 36, cornerRadius = 10, fill = "#E2E8F0" })
  Insert(m, { type = "frame", name = "Thumb", x = (i - 1) * 100 + 2, y = 2, width = 96, height = 32, cornerRadius = 8, fill = "#FFFFFF" })
  labels[i] = {}
  for j, name in ranges do
    labels[i][j] = Insert(m, { type = "text", name = name, content = name, x = (j - 1) * 100 + 32, y = 9, fontSize = 14, fontWeight = if i == j then 600 else 400, fill = "#0F172A" })
  end
  members[i] = { props = { Range = active }, node = m }
end
MakeVariants("Range", members)
for i in ranges do
  for j, name in ranges do
    if i ~= j then Link(labels[i][j], { Range = name }, "smart", { easing = "quick" }) end
  end
end

G.browse = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Browse", x = 440, y = 4400, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", padding = 24, gap = 16 })
Insert(G.browse, { type = "instance", ref = G.tabs[1], name = "Tabs" })
Insert(G.browse, { type = "instance", ref = Variant("Range", { Range = "Day" }), name = "Range" })
local run = Simulate(G.browse, { "click Tabs/Search", "click Range/Month", "click Range/Week" })
local shows = {}
for _, c in run.components or {} do shows[c.layer] = c.variant end
assert(shows["Browse/Tabs"] == "Tab=Search" and shows["Browse/Range"] == "Range=Week", "tabs and the segmented control")
```

## Variables

A prototype can remember things: a cart count, whether you're signed in, a quiz score, dark mode. A **variable is a Token** (number, text, true/false or colour); its modes are a Theme axis. Clicks change them, layers show them, and `if` decides with them.

- **Make them:** `SetVars({ count = 0, signedIn = false, name = "Ann", accent = "#EF4444" })`. The type comes from the value. A value per mode is a list: `{ { value = 1, theme = { product = "a" } }, { value = 5, theme = { product = "b" } } }`. `null` deletes. They're ordinary Tokens, so `GetTokens` and the Variables panel show them.
- **Read them:** `GetVars()` → `{ count = { type = "number", value = 0 }, … }` (`values` too when it has modes); `GetVars("count")` for one.
- **Bind layers to them** with `"$name"`:
  - text: `content = "$count"` (a number shows as text: `3`, `3.99`, at most 2 decimals);
  - visibility: `enabled = "$signedIn"` (hidden layers leave the auto layout, which closes up);
  - an Instance's variant: `props = { LoggedIn = "$signedIn" }` (picks the member; see Interactive components). It's **two-way**: a Change to on that Instance writes the variable;
  - any colour or number: `fill = "$accent"`, `width = "$barWidth"`.
  
  Only a whole `"$name"` binds; there are no text templates. For "3 items", put `$count` and `items` side by side in a row, or set a text variable with an expression.
- **Change them** (actions in `interactions`, run in order like any others):
  - `{ action = "set", var = "count", to = 0 }`: a literal (`"Ann"`, `true`, `"#EF4444"`, `"$other"` copies another);
  - `{ action = "set", var = "count", to = "{count + 1}" }`: a string that is exactly `"{…}"` is an expression;
  - `{ action = "toggle", var = "signedIn" }` flips a boolean;
  - `{ action = "mode", axis = "theme", value = "dark" }` switches the page's mode (Figma's Set variable mode). Layers with their own `theme` keep it;
  - `{ action = "if", cond = "{email != '' and agree}", ["then"] = { … }, ["else"] = { … } }`. `else` is optional; else-if is an `if` inside `else`.
- **Expressions:** `+ - * /`, unary `-`, `== != < <= > >=`, `and or not`, brackets, numbers, `'text'`, `true`/`false`. Text `+` joins, and a number joins as text (`'Hi ' + name`, `'' + score + ' / 5'`). Names with `/` or `-` go in square brackets: `{[cart/count] + 1}`. `count:pears` reads one mode's value (`count:product=pears` when the value name is on two axes). No functions and no `&&`/`||`. Division by zero gives 0 and a `div-zero` problem.
- **Try one:** `Eval("{count + 1}", { count = 2 })` → `3`. Other names read the Design's values; `Eval("price", nil, { mode = { product = "pears" } })` reads a mode. It returns `value, problems`; a bad expression fails the call with the reason.
- **Lifetime (as Figma):** values live for the whole playing session, through navigate, Back, overlays and component resets. **Only Restart** (R in Play, `"restart"` in Simulate) puts them, and the mode, back. A set just before a navigate already shows on the new Screen's first frame.
- **No animation:** a variable change snaps, as in Figma. An action that animates (navigate, Change to, overlay) holds the actions after it until it ends, so a mode set after a 300 ms Change to lands at 300 ms.
- **Per mode:** a set writes the value of the mode the clicked layer sees. A `+1` button inside a card with `theme = { product = "pears" }` changes only pears' value.
- **Simulate with variables:**
  - steps `"set count=3"`, `"set name='Ann'"`, `"set n={n * 2}"`, `"mode theme=dark"` (or `{ set = { count = 3 } }`, `{ mode = { theme = "dark" } }`), in the page's mode;
  - start values: `Simulate(start, steps, { vars = { count = 7 }, mode = { theme = "dark" } })`; Restart goes back to these;
  - the result has `vars` (only the variables that differ from the Design's; a per-mode change is a list of `{ value, theme }`) and `mode` (once one is switched). Steps say what ran: `"set count = 3"`, `"if count > 0: then"`, `change "Switch" to …, which sets dark = true`;
  - problems: `div-zero`, `var-type`, `var-unknown`, `expr-invalid`, `mode-unknown`.
  - a click whose `if` takes an empty branch (`−` at 0 in a clamp) isn't a `no-op`: the step's `notes` say `nothing changed: if cartCount > 0 was false, and that branch is empty`. Read the notes when a guard should have let the click through.
- **Lint** warns about `var-unknown`, `var-type` (a set or binding of the wrong type; a condition that isn't true/false), `mode-unknown` and `expr-invalid`, and notes `var-unused`.
- Play has a **Variables** popover (the values now, changed ones marked, and Reset). The HTML export plays variables too.

**A cart counter.** `+` and `−` (clamped at 0 with an `if`), and a badge in the tab bar of every Screen, kept across navigation:

```luau
SetVars({ cartCount = 0 })
local function tabBar(screen)
  local bar = Insert(screen, { type = "frame", name = "Tab bar", x = 0, y = 764, width = 390, height = 80, fill = "#FFFFFF",
    layout = "horizontal", justifyContent = "space_around", alignItems = "center" })
  local shopTab = Insert(bar, { type = "text", name = "Shop tab", content = "Shop", fontSize = 15, fill = "#0F172A" })
  local cartTab = Insert(bar, { type = "frame", name = "Cart tab", layout = "horizontal", gap = 6, alignItems = "center", children = {
    { type = "text", name = "Label", content = "Cart", fontSize = 15, fill = "#0F172A" },
    { type = "frame", name = "Badge", layout = "horizontal", padding = { 2, 8 }, cornerRadius = 10, fill = "#DC2626", children = {
      { type = "text", name = "Count", content = "$cartCount", fontSize = 12, fontWeight = 700, fill = "#FFFFFF" } } } } })
  return shopTab, cartTab
end
G.shop = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Shop", x = 0, y = 6000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
G.cart = Insert({ type = "frame", name = "Cart", x = 440, y = 6000, width = 390, height = 844, fill = "#F8FAFC", layout = "vertical", padding = 32, gap = 16 })
Insert(G.shop, { type = "frame", name = "Plus", width = 56, height = 44, cornerRadius = 10, fill = "#4F46E5",
  interactions = { { trigger = "click", actions = { { action = "set", var = "cartCount", to = "{cartCount + 1}" } } } } })
Insert(G.shop, { type = "frame", name = "Minus", width = 56, height = 44, cornerRadius = 10, fill = "#E2E8F0",
  interactions = { { trigger = "click", actions = { { action = "if", cond = "{cartCount > 0}",
    ["then"] = { { action = "set", var = "cartCount", to = "{cartCount - 1}" } } } } } } })
for _, s in { G.shop, G.cart } do
  local shopTab, cartTab = tabBar(s)
  Link(shopTab, G.shop, "dissolve")
  Link(cartTab, G.cart, "dissolve")
end
local run = Simulate(G.shop, { "click Plus", "click Plus", "click Plus", "click Minus", "click Cart tab" })
assert(run.screen.name == "Cart" and run.vars.cartCount == 2, "2 in the cart, shown on Cart")
run = Simulate(G.shop, { "click Minus", "click Plus", "restart" })
assert(run.vars == nil, "clamped at 0, and Restart resets")
assert(run.steps[1].notes and run.steps[1].notes[1]:find("branch is empty"), "the clamp click is a note, not a problem")
```

